UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 34, Lesson 3: The language of comparison', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"The language of comparison uses markers (similarly, likewise, whereas, unlike, in contrast) to show how ideas relate.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nAdd a comparison marker to link: The goat is greedy. The bird shares.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUnlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy.\n\nCHECK\nJumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite ten comparison sentences using at least five different markers. Label whether each shows likeness or difference.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"The language of comparison uses markers (similarly, likewise, whereas, unlike, in contrast) to show how ideas relate.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-VOCAB-01","EN2-SPELL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write ten comparison sentences using at least five different markers. Label whether each shows likeness or difference. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy. Reasoning: I used comparison by attending to similarly rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship. Correction: Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy. Why: the correction restores accurate use of comparison.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating comparison on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with comparison, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy. This matches the lesson skill for comparison.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy.","Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship."],"question":"Add a comparison marker to link: The goat is greedy. The bird shares. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship.","The language of comparison uses markers (similarly, likewise, whereas, unlike, in contrast) to show how ideas relate.","Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy.","Careful use of similarly with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising comparison?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named similarly, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the similarly used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising comparison. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries comparison.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show comparison and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship."],"question":"Read: \"When the ferry horn blasted, Omar gripped the railing and laughed.\" Which choice best uses comparison from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about comparison?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise similarly?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about comparison.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of comparison to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating comparison on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy. Reasoning: I used comparison by attending to similarly rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with comparison, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship. Correction: Unlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy. Why: the correction restores accurate use of comparison.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w34-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise comparison.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use comparison with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify similarly and explain why it matters.","I can practise whereas independently on a new prompt.","I can notice unlike needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The language of comparison uses markers (similarly, likewise, whereas, unlike, in contrast) to show how ideas relate. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["The language of comparison uses markers (similarly, likewise, whereas, unlike, in contrast) to show how ideas relate.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about comparison?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Unlike the crow, the ant prepares early. Similarly, both learn a cost for poor choices.\" Markers guide the reader.\n\nTRY FIRST\nAdd a comparison marker to link: The goat is greedy. The bird shares.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUnlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy.","explain":"The language of comparison uses markers (similarly, likewise, whereas, unlike, in contrast) to show how ideas relate.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Comparison language markers.","caption":"Show how ideas relate.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Likeness</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">similarly / likewise</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Difference</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">unlike / whereas</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"Unlike the crow, the ant prepares early. Similarly, both learn a cost for poor choices.\" Markers guide the reader. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"Unlike the crow, the ant prepares early. Similarly, both learn a cost for poor choices.\" Markers guide the reader. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about comparison?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Unlike the crow, the ant prepares early. Similarly, both learn a cost for poor choices.\" Markers guide the reader. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Marked comparison vs unmarked jumps.","caption":"Use five marker types.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Label</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">likeness or difference</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no markers between texts</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about comparison?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship. Here is a more productive model: \"Unlike the crow, the ant prepares early. Similarly, both learn a cost for poor choices.\" Markers guide the reader.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUnlike the goat, the bird shares. / The bird shares; in contrast, the goat is greedy.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Jumping between texts with no markers confuses the relationship. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about comparison?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe language of comparison uses markers (similarly, likewise, whereas, unlike, in contrast) to show how ideas relate. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 34, Lesson 3: The language of comparison","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Folktales and world stories compared","week":34,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"Unlike the crow, the ant prepares early. Similarly, both learn a cost for poor choices.\" Markers guide the reader.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Comparison language markers.","caption":"Show how ideas relate.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Likeness</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">similarly / likewise</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Difference</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">unlike / whereas</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Marked comparison vs unmarked jumps.","caption":"Use five marker types.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Label</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">likeness or difference</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no markers between texts</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y4-w34-l3' AND family_id IS NULL;
